From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Schwender <schwenderjonathan@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 10:44:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wpt: Condition expectations on the JavaScript engine

Expectations can now differ for QuickJS builds with conditions like
`if js_engine == "quickjs": FAIL`, as upstream's do for the Vello canvas
subsuite. Expectations without such a condition are SpiderMonkey's.

- `mach test-wpt --js-engine` (default: js-engine in .servobuild) sets
  `js_engine` in the run info. Every run sets it, because a condition on
  a property that the run info lacks raises a KeyError.
- `mach update-wpt` adds `js_engine` to the update properties. It reads
  them from the product module, so `--extra-property` doesn't work there.
  It wraps two wptrunner functions instead of patching the vendored code.
- An update from QuickJS logs alone would make their results the default
  for both engines. So every QuickJS result without a SpiderMonkey result
  in the logs gets the SpiderMonkey result of the current expectations.
- Files with results of both engines are updated with intermittent
  statuses: the value of a new condition is computed without the current
  one, which turned SpiderMonkey's `[OK, TIMEOUT]` into OK.
- Logs without `js_engine` count as SpiderMonkey runs.

Folded in "wpt: Keep both engines' expectations in update-wpt":

Fixes from an independent review of the per-engine update:

- `--full` rebuilds the conditions from the logs, so with the logs of
  one engine it dropped the other's expectations. It now refuses
  unless the logs have both engines.
- Whether the logs have a SpiderMonkey result for the configuration of
  a QuickJS result compared the whole run info, so logs of different
  revisions synthesized a SpiderMonkey result from the old
  expectations next to the new one and recorded `[PASS, FAIL]`. It now
  compares the update properties only.
- Intermittent statuses are updated only in files where SpiderMonkey
  has intermittent or differing results next to QuickJS results,
  instead of every file with QuickJS results.

Validation: with hand-written logs, `./mach update-wpt` wrote `if js_engine == "quickjs": FAIL` for a QuickJS-only subtest failure without touching SpiderMonkey's expectations, wrote the inverse condition where QuickJS passes a subtest SpiderMonkey fails, restored upstream's file byte for byte when QuickJS matched SpiderMonkey again, and updated only the default from a log without `js_engine`. On the logs of a full QuickJS run (next patch), every changed file evaluates the same for SpiderMonkey as before. `./mach test-wpt --js-engine quickjs` logged `js_engine: quickjs` and a SpiderMonkey run `spidermonkey`. `./mach test-scripts` and `./mach test-tidy` pass.
Validation: ("wpt: Keep both engines' expectations in update-wpt") `./mach test-scripts` passes, with a new test case for the per-engine update; regenerating the QuickJS expectations from the logs behind them gives the same files, apart from the serializer flattening the `prefs` lists of two files; `./mach test-tidy` passes.
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; written by the agent, which also ran the checks above)
AI-assisted: Yes (agent session; "wpt: Keep both engines' expectations in update-wpt": from an independent agent review, written by the agent)
